WEBVTT

1
00:00:03.400 --> 00:00:07.000
Why can swapping which parent has white eyes change every son?

2
00:00:08.440 --> 00:00:12.580
Morgan's reciprocal fly crosses looked almost identical at the start.

3
00:00:13.920 --> 00:00:15.060
Their offspring did not.

4
00:00:16.200 --> 00:00:20.020
The difference points to a chromosome route, not a new inheritance law.

5
00:00:21.033 --> 00:00:22.453
Start inside the flies.

6
00:00:23.813 --> 00:00:25.733
A female has a pair of X chromosomes.

7
00:00:27.133 --> 00:00:34.213
A male has an X and a Y. The white gene sits on the X, not the Y. Gene location

8
00:00:34.213 --> 00:00:38.033
decides which chromosome carries an allele through egg or sperm.

9
00:00:39.133 --> 00:00:42.793
A male fly has one X, so he is hemizygous for white.

10
00:00:44.093 --> 00:00:45.613
His Y carries no white gene.

11
00:00:46.933 --> 00:00:49.793
A recessive white allele therefore shows in a male.

12
00:00:51.133 --> 00:00:53.053
No dominant allele can mask it.

13
00:00:54.133 --> 00:00:58.073
A female can carry white without showing it when a red allele is present.

14
00:00:59.167 --> 00:01:02.007
Now follow the chromosomes, not the eye colours.

15
00:01:03.727 --> 00:01:07.767
Sons receive their only X from their mother, while daughters receive their father's

16
00:01:07.767 --> 00:01:11.727
X. The father's Y makes the other route into sons.

17
00:01:13.087 --> 00:01:16.667
That unequal route is the key to the reciprocal-cross puzzle.

18
00:01:17.750 --> 00:01:21.110
Begin with a red-eyed mother carrying only red alleles.

19
00:01:22.390 --> 00:01:27.990
Every child receives her red X. The white-eyed father's X reaches daughters, where

20
00:01:27.990 --> 00:01:29.350
her red allele masks it.

21
00:01:30.610 --> 00:01:32.010
Every offspring is red-eyed.

22
00:01:33.290 --> 00:01:37.530
The daughters carry white, but their red maternal allele determines eye colour.

23
00:01:38.650 --> 00:01:40.990
Reverse only the parental eye colours.

24
00:01:42.230 --> 00:01:45.730
The mother now carries white alleles, and the father carries red.

25
00:01:46.950 --> 00:01:50.450
Before the chromosomes move, which offspring will have white eyes?

26
00:01:56.417 --> 00:02:02.457
Every daughter gets the father's red X and a maternal white X. She is red-eyed.

27
00:02:03.777 --> 00:02:09.337
Every son gets a maternal white X and the father's Y. He is white-eyed.

28
00:02:10.697 --> 00:02:14.377
This is criss-cross inheritance: father to daughter, mother to son.

29
00:02:15.367 --> 00:02:18.507
The two crosses use the same red and white alleles.

30
00:02:19.767 --> 00:02:21.327
Only the carrier's sex changed.

31
00:02:22.547 --> 00:02:24.027
Yet their first offspring differ.

32
00:02:25.427 --> 00:02:30.547
Reciprocal crosses can separate X-linked inheritance from ordinary autosomal

33
00:02:30.547 --> 00:02:36.767
inheritance. An ordinary autosomal allele would not change routes when the carrier's

34
00:02:36.767 --> 00:02:37.767
sex changes.

35
00:02:38.567 --> 00:02:41.527
This pattern does not replace Mendel's segregation.

36
00:02:42.987 --> 00:02:45.547
It adds chromosome location to the bookkeeping.

37
00:02:47.147 --> 00:02:52.087
Inheritance patterns depend on allele identity, chromosome location, and parental

38
00:02:52.087 --> 00:02:55.807
origin. Here, location changes the route.

39
00:02:57.267 --> 00:03:01.307
Parent of origin matters here because sex determines the chromosome route.

40
00:03:02.350 --> 00:03:04.910
Read the family board from the son backward.

41
00:03:06.230 --> 00:03:07.530
His X points to his mother.

42
00:03:08.770 --> 00:03:11.890
Read a daughter backward, and one X points to each parent.

43
00:03:13.210 --> 00:03:16.590
Swapping the carrier therefore changes where the allele can go.

44
00:03:17.750 --> 00:03:22.350
The allele itself behaves the same; its possible destinations have changed.
